    <briefdescription>
<para>Requirements are checks that stop the test case when they fail. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>When a precondition of the following checks does not hold, the test case cannot continue meaningfully. A failed requirement is reported and counted like a failed expectation, and then the rest of the test case body is skipped; the run continues with the next test case.</para>
<para>This is the behaviour of the fatal assertions of most C++ test frameworks (<computeroutput>ASSERT_*</computeroutput> in GoogleTest, <computeroutput>REQUIRE()</computeroutput> in Catch2 and doctest, <computeroutput>BOOST_REQUIRE()</computeroutput> in Boost.Test). The test case is stopped with an exception; without exceptions, it cannot be stopped alone, so the results of the test case are reported, ending with the failed requirement (followed, with the TAP reporter, by a <computeroutput>Bail out!</computeroutput> line), and the whole test execution is aborted with <computeroutput>runner::abort()</computeroutput>, as Catch2 does; the remaining test cases do not run, and no totals are written.  </para>
    </detaileddescription>
